  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 147 PM Saturday... \n\nKEY MESSAGE 1... Overall light rain is expected tonight through the \nday tomorrow as a pair of low pressure systems move north through \nthe region and along the coast.\n\nA stationary front is still located to our south, over northern \nFlorida. Looking at satellite and observations, there are a few \nwaves of low pressure located along the frontal boundary. A \nshortwave trough should carry these lows northward today through \nSunday, however models are still struggling with the evolution and \ntracks of the two main low pressure systems. The first low is \nlocated over the Big Bend of Florida and has a large and broad area \nof showers and more stratiform rain associated with it, located \nmostly over Georgia and into South Carolina. This rain should start \nto approach the region later today as the low moves northeast and \nweakens, however there is a deep layer of dry air that will have to \nmoisten before any rain reaches the surface. The second low is \ncurrently located further east, well offshore in the Atlantic. This \nlow should continue to strengthen as it shifts north off the NC \ncoast on Sunday. \n\nConfidence is still low on the precipitation amounts expected. \nDepending on the strength and location the offshore low tracks, \nportions of central NC may be deprived of moisture, reducing \nrainfall totals. It appears that many models are trending this way, \nwith a minimum in rainfall totals being shown in central NC and a \nmaximum over the mountains and along the coast. As of now, it looks \nlike the most likely solution places 0.25-0.5 inches of rain across \nthe CWA, with ensemble guidance still showing a wide range of \npossibilities. The 10th percentile of the LREF shows just a few \nhundredths of rain, while the 90th percentile shows 1-1.5 inch. The \nprobability of the higher end scenarios are low though, with the \nprobability of greater than 0.5 inch between about 35-50%, with the \nlargest percentages in the south and west. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... Below-normal temperatures are expected next week, \nfollowed by another potential low pressure system over the \nSoutheastern US.\n\nIn the wake of the coastal low this weekend, a mostly dry cold front \nis forecast to move sewd across the area Mon night. A Canadian high \nwill strengthen as it migrates ewd across the upr MS Valley, Great \nLakes, and Northeast US, ridging swd across central NC through mid-\nweek. As the high moves across the Northeast US and Canadian \nMaritimes Wed and Thu, another area of low pressure will develop \nalong/off the Southeast US coast and strengthen as it lifts nwd \nalong the East Coast. The low should lift away from the area Thu \nnight/Fri, with high pressure again building into the region in its \nwake. \n\nTemperatures: With the strengthening Canadian high ridging into the \narea Mon night-Wed, expect CAA from the NE and crashing low-level \nthicknesses. Highs will swing from well above normal on Monday, to \nnear/below normal Tue and below to well below by Wed. Temperatures \nshould rebound some Wed night through Fri as the low lifts along the \nCarolina and mid-Atlantic coasts. \n\nPrecipitation: The cold front passage Mon night should be largely \ndry, though a stray shower cannot be completely ruled out. The \nbetter chance for rain will be with the coastal low mid-week, with \nhighest chances south and east. However, there is still a great deal \nof uncertainty wrt where the heaviest rain/highest amounts will be \nand how long it will last. While there is still plenty of time for \nthe forecast to change, for now it appears earliest onset could be \non Wed, possibly holding off until Wed night, while highest chances \nare Wed night into Thu morn.",
